import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,ao as i}from"./chunks/framework.B2J7Oa2h.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Capability parity with drmTMB","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"drmtmb-parity.md","filePath":"drmtmb-parity.md","lastUpdated":null}'),r={name:"drmtmb-parity.md"};function s(n,e,d,l,c,g){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="Capability-parity-with-drmTMB" tabindex="-1">Capability parity with drmTMB <a class="header-anchor" href="#Capability-parity-with-drmTMB" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Capability parity with drmTMB {#Capability-parity-with-drmTMB}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>DRModels.jl is the Julia twin of R&#39;s <code>drmTMB</code>, a from-scratch distributional-regression engine (mean μ, scale <strong>σ</strong>, residual correlation <strong>ρ12</strong>) built around a sparse, exact-gradient Laplace substrate. This page says where DRModels.jl stands against <code>drmTMB</code>, and, separately, where the R↔Julia bridge (<code>engine = &quot;julia&quot;</code>) stands against DRModels.jl&#39;s own direct-Julia engine. <strong>ML is the default</strong> (REML likelihoods aren&#39;t comparable across fixed-effect structures); REML is opt-in.</p><p>Naming is kept stable across both packages: <code>sigma</code>, never <code>tau</code>, for scale; <code>rho12</code> for bivariate residual correlation. Group-level (phylo/spatial/study) covariance summaries are named structured-effect terms, not <code>rho12</code>.</p><p>Legend: ✅ available · 🔨 in progress · ⬜ planned · ⚡ DRModels.jl advantage.</p><h2 id="The-route-axis" tabindex="-1">The route axis <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-route-axis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The route axis {#The-route-axis}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A capability in <code>drmTMB</code>&#39;s R↔Julia ledger can be true in up to <strong>four different senses</strong>, and conflating them is the single easiest way to overclaim parity:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">Column</th><th style="text-align:left;">What it answers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>Native R</strong> (drmTMB/TMB)</td><td style="text-align:left;">Does the R package&#39;s own C++/TMB engine fit this model?</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>Direct Julia</strong> (DRModels.jl)</td><td style="text-align:left;">Does DRModels.jl&#39;s own engine fit this model, called directly from Julia?</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>R bridge</strong> (<code>engine = &quot;julia&quot;</code>)</td><td style="text-align:left;">Does calling <code>drmTMB(..., engine = &quot;julia&quot;)</code> from R reach DRModels.jl and return a result?</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>Inference evidence</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">What evidence exists that the <em>numbers</em> agree (coefficients, SEs, logLik, and separately assessed interval coverage)?</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>&quot;Covered&quot; in the drmTMB ledger is a capability claim, never a coverage claim.</strong> A row can be <code>claim_status = covered</code> (implemented, tested, documented, with point/SE/logLik parity evidence) while its interval-coverage fence stays separate. The R bridge has its own admission status and restrictions; strong direct-Julia evidence does not by itself qualify an R bridge route.</p><h2 id="Compared-routes" tabindex="-1">Compared routes <a class="header-anchor" href="#Compared-routes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Compared routes {#Compared-routes}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The twelve selected capabilities below are sourced from <code>drmTMB</code>&#39;s <code>inst/extdata/julia-capabilities.tsv</code>. The <strong>claim boundary</strong> column carries each row&#39;s evidence; several rows have no native R comparator at all, so their evidence route is simulation recovery or same-target native-vs-native agreement, not R-vs-Julia parity in the ordinary sense.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">Capability</th><th style="text-align:left;">Route (plain English)</th><th style="text-align:center;">Native R</th><th style="text-align:center;">Direct Julia</th><th style="text-align:center;">R bridge</th><th style="text-align:left;">Inference evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>base_gaussian_location_scale</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Gaussian location-scale, <code>bf(y~x, sigma~z)</code></td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅ supported</td><td style="text-align:left;">✅ coef/SE/logLik parity (2026-08-24/08-15)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>biv_gaussian_residual</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Bivariate Gaussian, residual <code>rho12</code></td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅ supported</td><td style="text-align:left;">✅ one-draw coef/SE/logLik parity, n=400</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>gaussian_phylo_mean</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Gaussian phylo random intercept on the mean</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">🔨</td><td style="text-align:left;">✅ phylo-SD agreement + measured mean-block coverage; sigma-axis solver defect open</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>gaussian_response_mask</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Missing Gaussian response, <code>response=&quot;include&quot;</code></td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅ supported</td><td style="text-align:left;">✅ include==drop equality, cross-engine <code>|Δlogℓik| = 4e-10</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>biv_q4_phylo_reml</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">q4 bivariate phylo location-scale, REML</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">🔨 experimental</td><td style="text-align:left;">✅ coef parity; measured coverage argues <strong>against</strong> promotion (off-diagonal phylocov under/over-covers)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>phylo_count_large_p</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Poisson/NB2 with large-p sparse phylo RE</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">🔨</td><td style="text-align:left;">✅ SE parity to p=3000 (199–295×); p=10,000 is DRModels.jl-only, no native comparator attempted</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>phylo_gamma_beta_binomial</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Gamma/Beta/Binomial with phylo RE</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">🔨</td><td style="text-align:left;">✅ native-vs-native coef/SE/logLik parity, one fixture</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>general_covariance_structured</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>relmat()</code> / relatedness K, Gaussian/Poisson/NB2/Gamma</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">🔨</td><td style="text-align:left;">✅ SE parity to 1e-7–1e-6, one seed/family</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>cross_family_latent</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Shared-latent mixed-family (e.g. Gaussian × Poisson)</td><td style="text-align:center;">⬜ <strong>no native comparator</strong></td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">🔨 (via drmTMB&#39;s own xfam bridge, not DRModels.jl&#39;s <code>bridge.jl</code>)</td><td style="text-align:left;">⚡ simulation recovery only; a parity claim is structurally impossible here</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>engine_control_surface</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>drm_control(optimizer=list(g_tol=…, algorithm=…))</code></td><td style="text-align:center;"><code>DRMControl(optimizer=:default/:lbfgs/:bfgs)</code> on qualified primary ML routes</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">🔨</td><td style="text-align:left;">🟡 Julia route-specific selector tested; R control-surface translation, profile/bootstrap forwarding and other routes remain open. No performance/interval claim.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>plain_binomial_nonphylo</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Fixed-effect binomial trials, no random effects</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅ supported</td><td style="text-align:left;">✅ logLik/coef <code>2.48e-13</code>, SE <code>1.27e-9</code> abs</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>location_scale_scale</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Location-scale-scale, <code>sd(group)</code>/<code>sd(group, phylogenetic)</code></td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">✅</td><td style="text-align:center;">🔨</td><td style="text-align:left;">✅ exact agreement across the Mizuno M2–M6q ladder (Δlogℓik = 0.000000)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>For these twelve selected rows, the source ledger records four R bridge routes as <code>supported</code> and eight as <code>experimental</code>. Ten have <code>claim_status = covered</code>; <code>cross_family_latent</code> is <code>partial</code>, and <code>engine_control_surface</code> is <code>experimental</code>. These statuses apply to each documented route and its limits, not every model in a family. The ledger also contains other capabilities. The R bridge remains Experimental as an overall interface; a <code>supported</code> route entry does not make every bridge input stable or supported. A mixed-family pair without a native R comparator cannot establish ordinary R-versus-Julia parity through simulation recovery alone.</p><h2 id="Default-marginal-approximation" tabindex="-1">Default marginal approximation <a class="header-anchor" href="#Default-marginal-approximation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Default marginal approximation {#Default-marginal-approximation}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>drmTMB&#39;s native engine integrates every random effect with TMB&#39;s Laplace approximation. DRModels.jl&#39;s default, <code>marginal = :LA</code>, means &quot;this route&#39;s own integrator&quot;, which is not always the Laplace approximation. On the main routes:</p><ul><li><p><strong>An ordinary random intercept <code>(1 | g)</code> on a non-Gaussian mean</strong> (Poisson, Binomial, NegBinomial2, Gamma, Beta, BetaBinomial, Student): per-group adaptive Gauss–Hermite quadrature with <strong>5 nodes per group</strong>, centred on each group&#39;s posterior mode. Tweedie uses 21 nodes and CumulativeLogit 41; LogNormal&#39;s single node is exact.</p></li><li><p><strong>A correlated <code>(1 + x | g)</code> on a non-Gaussian mean</strong>: the same adaptive quadrature, 5 nodes per axis.</p></li><li><p><strong>Gaussian <code>sigma ~ 1 + (1 | g)</code> and ZeroOneBeta <code>(1 | g)</code></strong>: a fixed 32-node Gauss–Hermite grid.</p></li><li><p><strong>Crossed intercepts <code>(1 | g) + (1 | h)</code>, and <code>phylo</code> / <code>relmat</code> / <code>animal</code> / <code>spatial</code> terms, on a non-Gaussian mean</strong>: the Laplace approximation, as in drmTMB (LogNormal&#39;s are exact, being Gaussian fits on log y).</p></li><li><p><strong>Gaussian random or structured effects on the mean, with no random effect on <code>sigma</code></strong>: the exact marginal, which the Laplace approximation also gives exactly. With the same <code>(1 | g)</code> on both the mean and <code>sigma</code>, the default is the Laplace approximation.</p></li></ul><p>On the adaptive-quadrature cells the two packages fit the same model with different integrators, so their log-likelihoods and estimates differ by more than rounding. On seven test datasets (Poisson, Beta, NegBinomial2, Gamma and Student random intercepts) the default and drmTMB log-likelihoods differed by 0.01 to 0.24 units and the estimates by up to about 3%. In every case drmTMB&#39;s value was the one further from a 61-node quadrature reference; the default stayed within about 5e-4 of it. A later Poisson <code>(1 | g)</code> comparison (2026-10-15, three simulated datasets) showed a smaller gap, about 0.0014.</p><ul><li><p><strong>To match drmTMB, pass <code>marginal = :Laplace</code>.</strong> On ten test datasets (Binomial included) the refitted log-likelihoods agreed with drmTMB&#39;s to within 5e-11 and the estimates to within 5.4e-8 (relative); in the 2026-10-15 Poisson comparison, the dataset checked agreed to 5.6e-9 in log-likelihood and 1e-11 in the estimates. <code>:Laplace</code> is accepted for one ordinary <code>(1 | g)</code> on the mean of Poisson, Binomial, NegBinomial2, Gamma and Beta (with <code>sigma ~ 1</code>) or Student, and for a Gaussian random intercept on <code>sigma</code> (<code>sigma ~ 1 + (1 | g)</code>, fixed-effect <code>sigma</code> covariates allowed) with a fixed-effect mean, by maximum likelihood. <code>LogNormal()</code> also accepts <code>:Laplace</code> for one ordinary mean random intercept: it returns the same exact single-node mode-centred quadrature fit as its default and records the requested label. Other LogNormal models refuse this selector. For families without a documented <code>:Laplace</code> route, use their default; a <code>marginal</code> keyword is not accepted by every family. Crossed and structured non-Gaussian routes generally use Laplace by default, with exact Gaussian calculations for the delegated LogNormal routes.</p></li><li><p><strong>Match the integration rule for the comparison.</strong> On the named adaptive- quadrature test cells, the default was closer to the 61-node reference. Extra nodes alone do not guarantee greater accuracy. Small groups and counts near zero can produce non-Gaussian group posteriors, so inspect sensitivity to the integration rule. Gaussian <code>sigma ~ 1 + (1 | g)</code> uses a fixed grid; its recorded large-group comparisons favoured <code>:Laplace</code> or <code>:AGHQ</code>. See the scale-axis row of <a href="./capabilities">What is tested</a>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Compare like with like.</strong> Use the same likelihood convention and approximation when comparing log-likelihoods, likelihood-ratio tests, AIC and BIC across these engines. For the random-effect cells discussed here, match the integration rule before making a cross-package comparison. <code>fit.marginal</code> records the option (<code>:LA</code>, <code>:Laplace</code>, <code>:AGHQ</code> or <code>:VA</code>; the default Gaussian fit with <code>(1 | g)</code> on both the mean and <code>sigma</code> is tagged <code>:Laplace</code>). <code>lrtest</code> and <code>anova</code> refuse a pair whose tags differ (a fit with no random effects, whose log-likelihood is exact, may be paired with any non-variational fit). <code>aic</code>, <code>bic</code> and <code>aicc</code> refuse a <code>:VA</code> fit but, taking one fit at a time, cannot check that two fits match. The tag is not the integrator: two default fits on different routes, such as <code>(1 | g)</code> against <code>(1 | g) + (1 | h)</code>, use different integrators, and no check catches that.</p></li></ul><p>The <a href="./model-guides/marginal-la-vs-va">Laplace vs variational marginals</a> guide covers the approximations in more depth.</p><h2 id="Performance" tabindex="-1">Performance <a class="header-anchor" href="#Performance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Performance {#Performance}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p><strong>Retained single-fit comparison:</strong> on the real q4_p100 dataset, using the same Laplace ML objective, DRModels.jl&#39;s direct-Julia engine (<code>fit_q4_sparse_tmb.jl</code>) converged in 1.14 s (logLik −256.51), while drmTMB took 2.48 s (logLik −256.52,<code>converged=false</code>code 8). The R fit&#39;s false convergence limits interpretation of the timing ratio. These measurements describe one machine and fixture on 2026-05-30; they do not establish current warm-workflow performance (see<code>report/comparison-grid.md</code>).</p></li><li><p><strong>O(p) sparse phylogenetic path (direct Julia only):</strong> a biological per-dimension-variance model scales at k≈1.08 (near-perfect O(p)) from p=100 to <strong>p=10,000</strong> (112.9 s), using a Takahashi sparse-precision sampler that never forms the dense Σ_phy. A separate paired head-to-head against drmTMB 0.6.0 on Totoro found the ratio crosses over near p≈1000 (Julia faster at p=100, drmTMB comparable-or-faster from p=1000 up). An earlier &quot;~12× at p=10,000&quot; figure was an <strong>extrapolation</strong> and has been retired.</p></li><li><p><strong>R-bridge warm-workflow performance is NOT yet measured (open gap, tracked informally as G5).</strong> No number in this repo compares a <em>warm</em> R-bridge call against native drmTMB or against DRModels.jl&#39;s own direct-Julia path.</p></li><li><p>A <strong>2026-09-01 q4 bridge fixture run</strong> exists in the repo&#39;s evidence trail but is <strong>execution evidence only</strong>; it used a mismatched control preset and a cold Julia start. It demonstrates the bridge round-trips a q4 fixture and returns a result; it makes <strong>no bridge speed claim</strong>, and should not be read as one.</p></li></ul><h2 id="Honest-gaps" tabindex="-1">Honest gaps <a class="header-anchor" href="#Honest-gaps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Honest gaps {#Honest-gaps}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p><strong>Every R-bridge route (<code>engine = &quot;julia&quot;</code>) is <code>experimental</code>.</strong> All twelve ledger rows carry that status regardless of how mature the underlying direct-Julia engine is; none has been promoted to a production-ready bridge claim.</p></li><li><p><strong>Profile-likelihood and bootstrap parity are unverified.</strong> This page does not establish cross-engine agreement or interval coverage for either method.</p></li><li><p><strong>q4 phylogenetic covariance correlations are derived-only.</strong> The scale-axis and cross-covariance entries of the q4 PLSM <code>Sigma_a</code> block have not been through a calibrated coverage campaign; measured evidence on <code>biv_q4_phylo_reml</code> shows diagonal mean-block entries near nominal but off-diagonal phylocov entries systematically miscalibrated (some under-covering, some uninformatively wide).</p></li><li><p><strong>No calibrated q4 profile/bootstrap coverage exists.</strong> Point/SE/logLik parity is measured on several q4-adjacent rows; interval coverage for profile or bootstrap CIs on the q4 engine is not.</p></li><li><p><strong>Package registration waits for v0.7.1 (D-183); CRAN is a separate, independently held gate.</strong> Neither this page nor any row on it implies registration or CRAN release timing, which are tracked and gated elsewhere and are not claims this page makes.</p></li></ul><hr><p>Sources: <code>drmTMB</code>&#39;s <code>inst/extdata/julia-capabilities.tsv</code> (12-row ledger, <code>r_bridge_status</code>/<code>drmjl_status</code>/<code>claim_status</code>/<code>claim_boundary</code> columns); this repo&#39;s <code>docs/design/capability-status.md</code> (direct-Julia implemented/rejected/planned/missing audit); this repo&#39;s <code>report/comparison-grid.md</code> (2026-05-30 engine × model × objective grid, the source of the retained single-fit and scaling figures). Compiled 2026-09-01. The default marginal approximation section was added 2026-10-16. Its seven- and ten-dataset figures come from this repository&#39;s class-1 Laplace parity comparison (2026-09-30, Poisson, Binomial, Beta, NegBinomial2, Gamma and Student random intercepts); its 2026-10-15 Poisson figures from a comparison of drmTMB at commit <code>ca3ddef</code> with DRModels.jl at commit <code>f71b292</code> (#1070).</p>',24)])])}const u=t(r,[["render",s]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
